BOOK = {'title': 'High Noon at the frontier town of Hope', 'author': 'Signature original', 'genre': 'Gunslinger Tales', 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Dust on the Wind', 'text': "the sheriff was the first to offer a meal and the news. 'the cattle baron means to take it all,' the sheriff said. 'The water, the grazing, the town. Unless somebody stands up.' Tom Beckett ate slowly. Standing up was a habit.\n\nTom Beckett rode into the frontier town of Hope at sundown, with a silver spur in the saddlebag and dust in every seam. The town had the feel of a held breath — and the reason was posted on every wall: the cattle baron's notice, claiming the valley.\n\nBut a silver spur changed everything. When Tom Beckett showed it — a silver spur, genuine, legal, undeniable — the room went quiet. It was proof. Proof that the cattle baron's claim was a lie, and lies, in the frontier town of Hope, have a short life."}, {'n': 2, 'title': 'The Claim', 'text': "The judge — the cattle baron's judge — set a hearing for Friday. Three days to prove a silver spur real, with the cattle baron's men watching every step. Tom Beckett smiled the thin smile of someone holding four aces.\n\nthe sheriff stood with Tom Beckett — and then the blacksmith, and the schoolteacher, and one by one half the town. 'We know Tom Beckett's word,' the sheriff said. It wasn't much. It was everything.\n\nthe sheriff dug up the records: the original survey, the territorial seal, the witness — old, afraid, but honest. a silver spur matched, link by link. the cattle baron's forgery claim started to look like what it was: desperation."}, {'n': 3, 'title': 'High Noon', 'text': "Friday noon. The street emptied the way streets do before a storm. Tom Beckett walked it alone — the sheriff had argued, but some walks you take solo. a silver spur was in the courthouse. This was about more than paper.\n\nthe cattle baron stepped out with two gunmen. 'Last chance,' the cattle baron said. 'Ride out.' Tom Beckett thought of the frontier town of Hope, of the sheriff, of every small place that ever needed one person to stand. 'No,' Tom Beckett said.\n\nthe cattle baron's claim died that afternoon, in public, on the record. the frontier town of Hope exhaled. the sheriff bought the drinks. Tom Beckett drank one, slow, tasting justice. It tasted like dust and sweet water."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "They reached the railhead with the herds and the deed and their heads high. the frontier town of Hope was solvent. the frontier town of Hope was free. Tom Beckett looked back at the dust of the trail and felt the peculiar ache of a job done.\n\nTom Beckett organized the drive: every ranch, every hand, moving the herds to the railhead themselves, cutting out the cattle baron's middlemen. the sheriff rode drag. a silver spur — the deed — rode in Tom Beckett's breast pocket, the reason for it all.\n\nthe cattle baron wasn't done — the breed never is. The cattle were driven off in the night, the water rights challenged, the railroad brought in. the frontier town of Hope needed more than a verdict; it needed a future."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "But Tom Beckett stayed through the winter, helping the sheriff mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of the frontier town of Hope instead of just its defender. It suited them.\n\nthe cattle baron was last seen heading east, smaller in the distance. the frontier town of Hope didn't gloat. The frontier doesn't gloat; it just keeps the water clean and the word good.\n\nthe frontier town of Hope threw a dance. the sheriff danced every dance. The schoolteacher cried. The blacksmith forged a silver spur's likeness in iron for the courthouse wall — 'so nobody forgets,' he said."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "Tom Beckett rides somewhere west, where the map goes blank. There are always more valleys, more villains, more folks needing one person to stand. The frontier doesn't end. It just moves.\n\ncourage at high noon. In the frontier town of Hope, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nthe sheriff keeps a room ready, and the kettle on, and the news of the frontier town of Hope in letters. 'Come home when the trail allows,' every letter ends. The trail always allows, eventually."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

